course: biology
lesson: 9
title: Coral reefs, rockpools and the seashore
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A coral reef looks like coloured rock, but it is built by billions of tiny animals called polyps, cousins of jellyfish, that make limestone skeletons and keep tiny algae inside their bodies to share food made from sunlight.
explain: Reefs are the busiest neighbourhoods in the sea: a clownfish lives in a stinging anemone, a little cleaner wrasse runs a cleaning station for big fish, and parrotfish crunch coral and turn it into white sand.
explain: The seashore is a harder home, because twice a day the tide goes out and leaves rockpool animals in sun, wind and beaks: limpets clamp down, anemones fold into blobs of jelly and crabs hide under weed.
explain: In Finding Nemo Marlin raises Nemo alone after Nemo's mum dies; real clownfish all hatch as males, and when the female of a group dies, the biggest male turns into the new female, so real Marlin would have become Nemo's mum.
explain: In SpongeBob SquarePants Patrick Star is famously brainless, and that is the one true joke: a real starfish has no brain at all, yet it walks on hundreds of tube feet and eats mussels with its stomach pushed out of its body.
fact: Clownfish live among a sea anemone's stinging tentacles, protected by a special mucus, and they clean the anemone and chase off fish that would nibble it.
fact: All clownfish hatch as males, and the biggest one in a group can turn into the female.
fact: Corals are tiny animals called polyps, related to jellyfish, that build limestone skeletons and keep algae inside that make food from sunlight, so reef corals need clear, sunny, shallow water.
fact: The Great Barrier Reef off Australia is about 2,300 km long and is the largest structure built by living things.
fact: When the sea gets too warm, corals push out their algae and turn white, which is called bleaching; without the algae they starve if the heat lasts too long, but they can recover if the water cools in time.
fact: Parrotfish scrape algae off coral with beak-like teeth, grind up coral rock and pass it out as fine sand, and one large parrotfish can make hundreds of kilograms of sand a year.
fact: Cleaner wrasse pick parasites and dead skin off big fish, even inside their mouths, and the big fish does not eat its cleaner, so both win.
fact: Starfish move on hundreds of tiny tube feet worked by water pressure, push their stomach into a mussel's shell to digest it, can regrow lost arms, and have no brain.
fact: At low tide beadlet anemones pull in their tentacles and become jelly blobs to keep moist; they sting prey with tiny harpoon cells, but too weakly to hurt a person's fingers.
fact: Crabs walk sideways because their leg joints bend best that way, though many can also walk slowly forwards, have 10 legs counting the 2 claws, and grow by moulting their hard shell.
fact: Limpets graze algae under water and clamp tight when the tide goes out, return to their own home scar on the rock, and their teeth are one of the strongest natural materials known.
fact: The Portuguese man o' war is not a jellyfish but a colony of tiny animals floating under a gas-filled sail, with stinging tentacles that can trail over 10 metres; even a dead one on the beach can sting.
fact: Seaweeds are algae with no roots, only a holdfast gripping the rock, and bladder wrack's air bubbles lift it towards the light when the tide is in.
fact: In seahorses the female puts her eggs into the male's belly pouch and the father gives birth, sometimes to over a thousand babies at a time.
exercise: How can a clownfish live among a sea anemone's stinging tentacles? | A special mucus on its skin stops the anemone stinging it | It is too fast to be stung, darting in and out between the tentacles | It has armour like a crab | It only touches the tentacles at night, when they are fast asleep
exercise: What does the clownfish do for its anemone? | Cleans it and chases off fish that would nibble it | Brings it sand and little shells to build a hard shell around itself | Gives it light from its stripes | Nothing, it only takes and gives nothing back
exercise: What is a coral reef made by? | Tiny animals called polyps building limestone skeletons | Rocks pushed up from the seabed by underwater volcanoes long ago | Shells washed together by the waves | Seaweed slowly turned to stone
exercise: Why do reef corals need clear, sunny, shallow water? | The algae inside them make food from sunlight | Corals like to be warm, just like people sunbathing on a beach | Deep water is too salty for them | They need to see the fish they eat
exercise: What is the largest structure built by living things? | The Great Barrier Reef | The Great Wall of China | A termite mound in Africa | A huge beaver dam in Canada
exercise: What is coral bleaching? | Corals turning white after pushing out their algae when the sea is too warm | Corals being scraped and cleaned white by hungry parrotfish | Corals being painted white by divers | Corals turning white in winter like Arctic foxes, then back again in spring
exercise: Can bleached coral recover? | Yes, if the water cools in time | No, bleached coral is always dead at once | Only if it is painted again | Only if parrotfish eat it
exercise: What does a parrotfish turn coral into? | Fine white sand | Coral-coloured pearls | Seaweed | Rock that sinks to the deep
exercise: Why doesn't a big fish eat the cleaner wrasse in its mouth? | The wrasse cleans off its parasites, so both win | The wrasse is poisonous and tastes horrible | The big fish has no teeth | The wrasse is far too small for the big fish to see
exercise: How does a starfish eat a mussel? | It pulls the shell open a crack and pushes its stomach inside | It crushes the shell with its teeth | It swallows the mussel whole, shell and all, then spits the shell out | It waits for the mussel to die and open
exercise: What does a starfish walk on? | Hundreds of tiny tube feet | Five long legs with bendy knees | Its sharp spines, like stilts | Nothing, it cannot move at all
exercise: Why does a beadlet anemone become a blob of jelly at low tide? | Pulling its tentacles in keeps it from drying out | It is asleep until the moon comes up | It has died, and comes back to life when the tide returns | It is pretending to be a sweet for gulls
exercise: Why do crabs usually walk sideways? | Their leg joints bend best that way | They cannot see straight forwards at all | Their shells are too wide for narrow gaps | They are following the tide line along the beach
exercise: Why do limpets clamp so hard to the rock when the tide goes out? | To stop drying out and being knocked off or eaten | To drink water from inside the rock | Because they are glued there for life, from the day they hatch | To keep warm in the cold air
exercise: Why should you never touch a Portuguese man o' war on the beach? | Even a dead one can sting badly | It bites with its tiny hidden teeth | It is protected by law and must never be moved | Its slime makes skin numb for ever
exercise: Why does bladder wrack have air bubbles? | They float it up towards the light when the tide is in | They store fresh water for dry days | They are eggs about to hatch into new seaweed plants | They help it breathe like lungs
exercise: Who carries the babies in seahorses? | The father, in a pouch on his belly | The mother, kept safe inside her mouth | Nobody, the eggs float free in the sea | Both parents in turn, on their backs
test: «finding nemo: dad raises nemo alone, so that's what clownfish do» Which is closer to the truth? | When the female dies the biggest male turns female, so Marlin would become the mum | Clownfish dads always raise babies alone | Clownfish mums never die | Clownfish babies raise themselves in the anemone from day one, without any parents at all
test: «patrick star's dumb cos he's a starfish lol» Which is the true part? | A real starfish has no brain at all | Starfish have huge brains | Starfish have a brain in each arm | Starfish are fish, so they have fish brains
test: Count them: three crabs, each with 2 claws and 8 walking legs. How many legs altogether, counting claws? | 30 | 24 | 18 | 33
test: Count them: a starfish lost 1 of its 5 arms and two other starfish have all 5. How many arms in the rockpool? | 14 | 15 | 10 | 13
test: What would happen to a coral reef if the sea stayed too hot for a very long time? | The corals would bleach, starve and die | The corals would grow faster and bigger | The corals would move to deeper water | Nothing, corals love heat
test: «dead jellyfish thing on the beach, gonna poke it with my finger» What is the best advice? | Don't: a Portuguese man o' war can sting even when dead; tell a lifeguard | Fine, dead ones can't sting | Fine, just rinse your finger in the sea | Pick it up and throw it back in the sea, where it can come back to life and swim away
test: «ugh this limpet's stuck, gimme a knife to get it off» Which is the best answer? | Leave it: it clamps to stay alive and returns to its own spot | Pull harder, it is only a stone stuck to another stone | Use a knife, they don't feel anything | Tap it, it always lets go
test: «white sandy beach so pretty» Which surprising fact is true? | Some white tropical beach sand comes partly from parrotfish poo | All beach sand is made by crabs grinding shells with their claws | White sand is made of salt | White sand is ground-up seaweed
test: «that coral's just pretty rocks innit» What is right? | Coral is built by living animals, the polyps | Yes, it is rock from old volcanoes | Yes, it is coloured stone | It is a kind of seaweed
test: «that seahorse dad's pregnant?! is that legit» What is right? | Yes, in seahorses the father carries the eggs in a pouch and gives birth | No, it is a female with a big tummy | No, seahorses lay eggs on rocks and then swim away | Yes, but only once in his life
test: «crabs walk sideways cos they're drunk» What is right? | Their leg joints bend best that way, though they can walk forwards slowly | They cannot walk forwards at all | They walk sideways only on sand | They are copying the waves
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a coral reef? | A coral reef is a structure of limestone constructed by colonies of minute animals termed polyps | A coral reef is a formation of volcanic rock | A coral reef is an accumulation of shells and pebbles deposited over many centuries by the action of waves | A coral reef is a mass of petrified seaweed
exam: Identify the principal cause of coral bleaching. | Elevated sea temperatures induce corals to expel the algae resident within their tissues | Excessive cleaning by parrotfish | The application of paint by divers | The onset of winter in tropical seas
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the sex of clownfish. | All clownfish hatch as males, and the largest individual of a group may become female | All clownfish hatch as females and remain so | The sex of clownfish is fixed for life at hatching and cannot alter under any circumstances | Clownfish possess no sex whatsoever
exam: Ascertain the aggregate number of legs, inclusive of claws, possessed by four crabs, each bearing two claws and eight walking legs. | Forty | Thirty-two | Eight | Thirty-six
